WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.520
Here are the stories of some of the most popular
shows about cool teenagers that aired in the

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.060
1990s — and the reasons they had to grow
up, move on, and leave the airwaves.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.280
Good Morning, Miss Bliss, a short-lived Disney
Channel vehicle for former child star Hayley

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.609
Mills about an Indiana teacher was revamped
by NBC into Saved by the Bell, a show about

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:23.410
supercool Bayside, California, teen Zack Morris.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.449
The fourth-wall-breaking, Ferris Bueller-like
character was supported in his schemes by

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:33.800
his inner circle: mega-nerd Screech, jock
A.C. Slater, fashionista Lisa Turtle, overachiever

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.109
Jessie Spano, and sometimes-girlfriend Kelly
Kapowski.

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:42.180
They also had a band called the Zack Attack
and hung out in a diner called the Max, run

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.579
by a magician.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:47.589
Saved by the Bell was kind of weird, but it
was a smash hit on NBC's Saturday morning

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.720
schedule from 1989 to 1993.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.320
So why did it have to end?

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:53.339
"I'm sorry, Zack.

00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:57.480
I wish there was a way we could still put
on the show so you could be in it."

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.960
The characters on the show aged in real time,
and four years is as long as high school lasts.

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:07.340
Not only were they scheduled to move on from
Bayside High, but Tiffani Thiessen and Elizabeth

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.120
Berkley left the show midway through the 1992/1993
season.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.520
NBC wasn't ready to say goodbye, so it launched
the primetime continuation Saved by the Bell:

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.409
The College Years, which found Zack out of
his element as a freshman at California University,

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:24.680
along with most of his high school friends.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.760
Viewers apparently weren't interested in a
non-Bayside High Bell, and the show bombed,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:36.090
ranking at #88 in the ratings for the 1993-'94
season… which was its only season.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.289
Undaunted, the studio launched yet another
spinoff, Saved by the Bell: The New Class,

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:45.740
which actually ended up outlasting every other
iteration of the show during its seven-season

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:46.780
run.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.189
Proving your high school years are always
a part of you, members of the original cast

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:56.080
reunited for a Saved by the Bell revival in
2020, streaming on NBC's Peacock platform.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:03.040
Beverly Hills, 90210 was arguably the starting
point and inspiration for all '90s teen culture.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.829
Not long after its premiere on Fox in 1990,
the soap became the hottest show on the air

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:10.440
for millions of young Americans.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.410
Fans needed to know what happened in the social
lives of Brandon and Brenda Walsh, Minnesota

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.840
transplants who moved to a certain ritzy Los
Angeles suburb, along with their various friends

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.760
and romantic partners.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.640
Brenda's relationship with Dylan became iconic
for millions of teens, who bought magazines

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.220
and posters featuring the show's photogenic
cast.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.060
Beverly Hills, 90210 proved so popular, and
vital to the rise and continued success of

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.010
Fox, that the network kept it on the air.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.370
It evolved from a high school show to a college
show, then simply one about the lives of young

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.560
adults.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.950
By the year 2000, ten years after its debut,
90210 was the longest-running drama on American

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.299
nighttime TV, although much of the original
cast had long since left and been replaced

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:54.880
by new, similarly good-looking people.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.550
With the show long in the tooth and its characters
nearing their 30s, Fox canceled the once-mighty

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.880
teen soap after nearly 300 episodes.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.910
Like many long-running series, Beverly Hills,
90210 spawned a series of spinoffs, the most

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:12.510
popular of which, Melrose Place, became one
of the defining shows of its era in its own

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:13.400
right.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.760
That tradition continued in 2008 with 90210,
a spinoff that focused on the lives of a different

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.939
group of teens in the ritzy area code; after
that show left the air in 2013, yet another

00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:30.159
series, BH90210, reunited the original cast
in 2019 — this time with the actors playing

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.600
fictionalized versions of themselves.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.880
It's an appealingly weird idea, but viewers
weren't interested: BH90210 left the airwaves

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.800
after only six episodes.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.440
"Now we get to sit around and wait to see
if we get picked up."

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.000
"That's a formality, right?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.720
I mean, it's the reboot of 90210, no brainer."

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.680
At the peak of Beverly Hills, 90210's success
came My So-Called Life, an extremely low-key

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.080
drama about a girl right in the middle of
the social chain struggling with adolescence

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.760
and her parents' expectations and perceptions
of her — stories told with copious narration

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.670
from 15-year-old Claire Danes, who starred
as quiet, introspective Angela Chase.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.129
"I've been kissed three times.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:13.049
No, four times.

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:14.049
No.

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:15.370
Three times."

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:20.110
The show became a cult hit and critical darling,
praised for its sensitive, non-condescending,

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:24.280
and realistic portrayal of teenage life, and
Danes became one of the youngest performers

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.430
to ever be nominated in a lead acting category
at the Emmy Awards.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:34.690
However, My So-Called Life was not even remotely
a hit, ranking as the 116th-most-watched show

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:40.970
of the 1994/1995 TV season and the least watched
program on ABC overall.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:45.390
That's probably because the network scheduled
it Thursdays at 8 against NBC's juggernaut

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.350
"Must See TV" sitcom lineup.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:52.130
My So-Called Life went head to head with Mad
About You and a brand-new sitcom called Friends,

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:56.980
which would become iconic for the generation
at whom My So-Called Life was directed.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.560
After one season, ABC canceled its groundbreaking
teen drama.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.960
When Clarissa Explains It All hit Nickelodeon
in 1991, it was like nothing else on TV at

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:06.980
the time.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.200
Melissa Joan Hart starred as adolescent Clarissa
Darling, who talked to the audience to explain

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.470
her life, the actions of others, and any other
number of quirks of the human existence.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:20.750
Clarissa became a role model for her creative
flair, abundant confidence, and wacky sense

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.170
of humor.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:25.880
Plus viewers could relate to the trials of
having goofy parents and an annoying little

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:26.970
brother.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.830
During Clarissa Explains It All's fourth season,
Nickelodeon decided that it wasn't going to

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.500
order more episodes.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.640
Why?

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:38.061
At 17, the character of Clarissa was too old
to be starring in a Nickelodeon show... but

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.920
not cool enough to star in a show on sibling
network MTV.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.480
Creator Mitchell Kriegman told Mental Floss:

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:49.530
"In those days, Nickelodeon stopped at 14
and MTV started at 15 or 16, and there was

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:50.530
no middle ground.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:54.901
They didn't cross that line, 'cause that was
MTV territory, and the attitude of MTV was

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:57.350
way different than the attitude of Nickelodeon."

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:02.060
Kriegman attempted to skip the demographic
pigeonholing altogether, and in 1995 pitched

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:06.720
to CBS a continuation called Clarissa, in
which the college-age title character lands

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.280
an internship at a New York newspaper.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.960
It never became a series.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.160
The early Fox network took some chances, airing
shows far edgier and more unusual than the

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.240
stuff seen on the other major broadcast networks.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:23.920
In 1990, it aired the surreal high school
comedy Parker Lewis Can't Lose.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.150
Later shortened to just Parker Lewis, the
show starred Corin Nemec as an extremely cool

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:31.920
teen doing constant battle with his principal
with the help of his friends: a rocker named

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.580
Mikey and a nerd named Jerry whose trench
coat pockets held a treasury of bizarre gadgets.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.960
Shot in the film-like single-camera format,
when studio audiences and laugh tracks were

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.140
the norm, Parker Lewis' quick cuts, weird
camera angles, and special effects certainly

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.680
put it ahead of its time.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.000
After three seasons, both show and network
were changing rapidly.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.520
Co-creator Clyde Phillips told The Hollywood
Reporter:

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.380
"The danger when you do a high school show
is that your characters have to grow and in

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.040
fact they have to grow out of high school."

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.280
As for Fox, the network was, quote, "looking
a bit to [...] get more adult," co-creator

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:08.500
Lon Diamond said, explaining that the network
was, at the time, aiming for more serious

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.830
teen content.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.280
Fox pulled the plug in 1993, after three seasons.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:18.280
Teenaged singer Brandy was everywhere in the
mid-'90s, releasing a slew of R&amp;B-tinged pop

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.470
smashes like "I Wanna Be Down," "Baby," and
"The Boy is Mine."

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:27.120
In 1996, UPN capitalized on Brandy's high
status with teenagers and gave her a sitcom,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.910
signing her to star on Moesha as Moesha, a
Los Angeles teen going through the normal

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.160
trials and tribulations of American adolescent
life.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:40.230
At a big event at New York's Madison Square
Garden in May 2001, UPN announced its big

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:44.320
moves for its slate of programming set to
debut the following fall.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.750
It was nothing short of an overhaul, what
with the cancellations of its two longest-running

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:53.050
shows: Star Trek: Voyager, which had been
on the air since 1995, and Moesha, ending

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.720
its five-year run.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.720
The reason?

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.240
Moesha had, quote, "run its course," according
to CEO Dean Valentine, who said:

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.700
"It was time to freshen up the night."

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.160
Getting rid of Moesha may have also been a
way to make room and set aside some cash.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:11.240
During the same media confab where the show
was officially canceled, UPN announced that

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:17.920
it had acquired two series from rival mini-network
The WB: Roswell and Buffy the Vampire Slayer.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.390
Most movies and teen shows about teens are
about the rich and popular kids.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:27.900
MTV's animated hit Daria had plenty of those
characters, except that they were a source

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:33.519
of derision for the heroes: brutally sarcastic
and proud social pariah Daria Morgendorfer

00:08:33.519 --> 00:08:36.439
and her best friend, aspiring painter Jane.

00:08:36.439 --> 00:08:40.579
The series brutally mocked high school and
all its social conventions, with Daria delivering

00:08:40.579 --> 00:08:45.399
her vicious and pointed criticisms at the
jocks, cheerleaders, and her popularity-obsessed

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:46.410
sister Quinn.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.290
"You can't go, you'll ruin everything!"

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:53.529
"You know, I really should broaden my social
horizons."

00:08:53.529 --> 00:08:58.339
Daria, which debuted in 1997 as a spinoff
of MTV's Beavis and Butthead, came about when

00:08:58.339 --> 00:09:01.480
the network needed a show to attract female
audiences.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:06.600
Showrunner Glenn Eichler was left mostly to
his own devices, and MTV offered him and his

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:10.139
team full creative freedom to make the show
they wanted.

00:09:10.139 --> 00:09:13.699
That also meant he got to end it on his own
terms when he was ready, and he wrapped up

00:09:13.699 --> 00:09:17.520
after five seasons with a TV movie about Daria's
graduation.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.279
But there's a good chance Daria wouldn't have
made it to another season, even if Eichler

00:09:21.279 --> 00:09:22.490
had wanted to do that.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:23.490
He told Variety:

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:27.699
"Not too long after the series ended, MTV
animation imploded, so I don't know if we

00:09:27.699 --> 00:09:29.640
would have even made it to a sixth year."

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.520
Ryan Murphy has created a lot of shows.

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:37.100
In addition to provocative stuff for grown-ups
like Nip/Tuck, Pose, and American Horror Story,

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.960
he's probably best known for getting Glee
on the air.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.720
But he honed his teen TV chops with his first
ever series, the darkly comic high school

00:09:44.731 --> 00:09:48.069
soap Popular, which hit The WB in 1999.

00:09:48.069 --> 00:09:51.940
It was about two very different teenagers,
cheerleader Brooke and school newspaper editor

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:55.720
Sam forced into togetherness when their respective
single parents marry.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.520
Popular seemingly fit right in with the WB's
late '90s lineup of teen fare like Dawson's

00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:05.829
Creek and Roswell, but Murphy later claimed
that there was a disconnect between his progressive

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:09.069
vision for the show and what the network wanted
it to be.

00:10:09.069 --> 00:10:10.600
He told Entertainment Weekly:

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.100
"They never got me and they kept trying to
turn me into something else.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.920
They would give me notes, like, 'The Mary
Cherry character, like, could she be less

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:18.800
gay?'"

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.680
"Even though I'm depressed and feel life has
no meaning since that editorial, this restroom

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.600
always makes me feel so pretty."

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.320
Overall, he, quote, "didn't have a good experience"
making Popular.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:32.920
Despite his following the network's every
suggestion, the show still got canceled after

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.120
two years anyway.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:40.749
The hit 1995 movie Clueless concerned a clothes-obsessed,
Beverly Hills teen named Cher Horowitz who

00:10:40.749 --> 00:10:45.000
just wants to get her driver's license, hang
out with her friend Dionn, and give new student

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.120
Tai a life-changing makeover.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:51.749
The cheery and casual dynamic, along with
the film's popularity, made a TV version almost

00:10:51.749 --> 00:10:56.870
a foregone conclusion, and Clueless the show
wound up on ABC's TGIF lineup in the fall

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:58.480
of 1996.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:02.480
Several cast members of the film reprised
their roles, while Rachel Blanchard took over

00:11:02.499 --> 00:11:04.880
for Alicia Silverstone as the lead.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.839
Despite moderately high ratings — it ranked
#53 for the year — ABC canceled it, only

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:12.660
for the teen-skewing UPN to seize it.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.580
The change in venue to the generally little-watched
smaller network hurt the show: viewership

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.920
fell, and after the third season of Clueless
saw another ratings drop, UPN canceled the

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:22.880
show.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.680
Buffy, a vampire slayer played by Sarah Michelle
Gellar, constantly faced danger, violence,

00:11:28.689 --> 00:11:33.390
and death in the line of duty — and Buffy
the Vampire Slayer the show outlasted almost

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:34.470
as many threats.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:39.600
It survived a rescinded cancellation, the
second death of the title character, a network

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:45.209
switch from the WB to UPN, the exit of popular
characters Angel and Cordelia to the spinoff

00:11:45.209 --> 00:11:51.029
Angel, and consistently poor ratings, never
ranking higher than 120th place in the annual

00:11:51.029 --> 00:11:52.720
Nielsen tally.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.879
"Huh?"

00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:59.339
Nevertheless, Buffy's crew produced 144 episodes
of what would become one of the biggest cult

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:03.790
shows of all time, chock full of action, stunts,
and serious acting.

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:08.379
That amounted to a tremendous grind for Gellar,
and by 2003, she'd had enough.

00:12:08.379 --> 00:12:09.709
"I quit.

00:12:09.709 --> 00:12:11.040
I resign.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:12.370
I'm fired."

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.060
She told Entertainment Weekly:

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.749
"I'm 26.

00:12:15.749 --> 00:12:16.749
I'm married.

00:12:16.749 --> 00:12:20.560
I never see my husband [...] You want to pick
up and go, try other things, live in different

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:21.689
places.

00:12:21.689 --> 00:12:24.319
It feels right, and you have to listen to
that."

00:12:24.319 --> 00:12:29.009
Without Buffy, there could be no Buffy, and
so UPN wrapped up the series.

00:12:29.009 --> 00:12:30.920
